# Orphan-instance mislinks for Bits and SplitPorts.
#
# Instances of Bits and SplitPorts are baked into generated hardware:
# they decide the bit layout and the Verilog pinout of every synthesized
# boundary that mentions the type.  An ORPHAN instance (class and all head
# types defined elsewhere) makes that choice a property of the IMPORT
# GRAPH instead of the type: two packages in one program, or two builds of
# one design, can resolve the same predicate differently, each locally
# coherently -- and the divergence surfaces only as silently mis-linked
# hardware.
#
# This suite asserts the DESIRED behavior: orphan instances of these two
# classes are rejected, so encodings and pinouts are a property of the
# type, program-wide.  Today bsc accepts every orphan below with at most a
# suppressible warning (T0127) at the definition site, so the assertions
# of desired behavior FAIL, in two flavors:
#
#   - "shouldn't compile" failures: the five orphan-defining packages
#     (WireEnc, WireEnc2, SplitFwd, SplitRev, ReqSplit) are accepted;
#   - damage witnesses: expected outputs and generated-Verilog assertions
#     below state the CORRECT results, and today's runs produce corrupted
#     data / divergent pinouts instead.
#
# Everything else PASSES, and that is the point: the passing assertions
# pin down that no diagnostic fires at any site where the divergent
# hardware is produced (and that the T0127/T0099/T0157 diagnostics fire
# only where they are useless or counterproductive).
#
# Expected failures against current bsc, in order:
#    1. WireEnc.bs   shouldn't compile        (orphan Bits accepted)
#    2. WireEnc2.bs  shouldn't compile        (orphan Bits accepted)
#    3. sysOrphanBitsMislink Verilog output   (roundtrip corrupted)
#    4. sysOrphanBitsMislink Bluesim output   (roundtrip corrupted)
#    5. TbOrphanBitsVeri.out                  (sink reads d5, not aa)
#    6. SplitFwd.bs  shouldn't compile        (orphan SplitPorts accepted)
#    7. SplitRev.bs  shouldn't compile        (orphan SplitPorts accepted)
#    8. TbOrphanSplitVeri.out                 (lanes crossed)
#    9. ReqSplit.bs  shouldn't compile        (orphan SplitPorts accepted)
#   10. mkReqSinkPlain.v pinout               (fell back to unsplit put_1)
#   11. sysBothSinks.v unsplit connection     (two pinouts in one program)
#
# (The suite demonstrates the gap; it is not the regression suite for a
# fix.  Under the proposed ban the five orphan-defining packages fail to
# compile, so the downstream tests become uncompilable rather than green.)

# ----------
# Shared protocol types (derived Bits layouts).  No diagnostics, correctly.
compile_pass_no_warning MsgDefs.bs

# Today the orphan encoders are merely warned about -- T0127, once, at the
# definition site, and nowhere else in this file.  (WireEnc moves valid to
# the MSB; WireEnc2 does the same and also swaps src/dst.  Each is a
# legal, strictly-more-specific overlap of the derived generic instance
# Bits (Msg t) _, so the duplicate check can never fire between an orphan
# and the derived instance.)
compile_pass_warning WireEnc.bs T0127 1
compile_pass_warning WireEnc2.bs T0127 1

# Desired: an orphan Bits instance is rejected.  FAILS today.
compile_fail WireEnc.bs
compile_fail WireEnc2.bs

# The damage sites.  NO diagnostics: each package's resolution of
# Bits (Msg Hdr) is locally unambiguous and coherent.
compile_pass_no_warning Ingress.bs
compile_pass_no_warning IngressB.bs
compile_pass_no_warning Egress.bs

# ----------
# CASE 1 (Bits, pure Bluespec): pack with the orphan layout in one package,
# ship the value over a type-erased channel (Reg (Bit 33)), unpack with the
# derived layout in another package.  The top-level compile sees BOTH
# instances and still has nothing to report (asserted PASS below).  The
# expected output states the correct roundtrip; today both backends compute
# silently corrupted data, so both simulation comparisons FAIL.
compile_pass_no_warning OrphanBitsMislink.bs
test_c_veri OrphanBitsMislink sysOrphanBitsMislink.out.expected

# The ONLY configuration the compiler can catch: both equal-head orphan
# cones imported into one compile (T0099, duplicate instances).  None of
# the failing flows in this file ever reach this configuration.
compile_fail_error OrphanBitsBothCones.bs T0099

# ----------
# CASE 2 (Bits, Verilog top level): two synthesized modules exposing the
# same logical type (Msg Hdr) on 33-bit ports, generated in different
# import cones, connected by a hand-written Verilog top exactly as any
# netlist-integration flow would.  Same names, same widths, different
# meanings: links cleanly, and the output comparison (which states the
# correct value) FAILS -- the sink reads d5 where the source drove aa.
if { $vtest == 1 } {
    compile_verilog_pass_no_warning MsgSource.bs mkMsgSource
    compile_verilog_pass_no_warning MsgSink.bs mkMsgSink

    # Same width on both sides of the boundary -- nothing for any tool to
    # object to.
    find_regexp mkMsgSource.v {output \[32 : 0\] out;}
    find_regexp mkMsgSink.v {input  \[32 : 0\] srcOf_1;}

    link_verilog_no_main_pass {TbOrphanBitsVeri.v mkMsgSource.v mkMsgSink.v} TbOrphanBitsVeri
    sim_verilog TbOrphanBitsVeri
    compare_file TbOrphanBitsVeri.out
}

# ----------
# CASE 3 (SplitPorts, Verilog top level): two orphan SplitPorts instances
# for the same vector type emit IDENTICAL port-name sets (_0.._3, same
# widths) with opposite lane mappings.  A module from each cone, joined by
# name in a Verilog top, silently crosses the lanes.
compile_pass_no_warning LaneDefs.bs

# Today: warned T0127 once, at the definition sites only.
compile_pass_warning SplitFwd.bs T0127 1
compile_pass_warning SplitRev.bs T0127 1

# Desired: an orphan SplitPorts instance is rejected.  FAILS today.
compile_fail SplitFwd.bs
compile_fail SplitRev.bs

# A plain (non-codegen) compile does not see the wrapper's use of the
# orphan, so the compiler warns that the import is UNUSED -- actively
# nudging the user to delete the import that determines the module's
# pinout.  (The codegen compiles below are warning-free.)
compile_pass_warning LaneSource.bs T0157 1
compile_pass_warning LaneSink.bs T0157 1

if { $vtest == 1 } {
    compile_verilog_pass_no_warning LaneSource.bs mkLaneSource
    compile_verilog_pass_no_warning LaneSink.bs mkLaneSink

    # The two pinouts are indistinguishable: identical names and widths.
    find_regexp mkLaneSource.v {output \[7 : 0\] out_0;}
    find_regexp mkLaneSource.v {output \[7 : 0\] out_3;}
    find_regexp mkLaneSink.v {input  \[7 : 0\] lane0_1_0;}
    find_regexp mkLaneSink.v {input  \[7 : 0\] lane0_1_3;}

    # The expected output states the correct (uncrossed) lane behavior;
    # today the name-based hookup crosses the lanes, so this FAILS.
    link_verilog_no_main_pass {TbOrphanSplitVeri.v mkLaneSource.v mkLaneSink.v} TbOrphanSplitVeri
    sim_verilog TbOrphanSplitVeri
    compare_file TbOrphanSplitVeri.out
}

# ----------
# CASE 4 (SplitPorts, pure Bluespec): the catch-all default instance
# (SplitPorts a (Port a)) means the ABSENCE of an orphan is never an error:
# the identical module body gets two different Verilog pinouts for the SAME
# interface type, depending only on the import cone, inside one program,
# with no diagnostic anywhere -- including in BothSinks, where both cones
# join.  Any constraints file, DFT flow, or netlist frozen against one
# pinout silently mismatches the other.
compile_pass_no_warning ReqDefs.bs

# Today: warned T0127 once, at the definition site only.
compile_pass_warning ReqSplit.bs T0127 1

# Desired: an orphan SplitPorts instance is rejected.  FAILS today.
compile_fail ReqSplit.bs

# As in CASE 3: the plain compile flags the pinout-determining import as
# unused; the module that silently falls back to the catch-all is silent.
compile_pass_warning SinkSplit.bs T0157 1
compile_pass_no_warning SinkPlain.bs
compile_pass_no_warning BothSinks.bs

if { $vtest == 1 } {
    compile_verilog_pass_no_warning SinkSplit.bs mkReqSinkSplit
    compile_verilog_pass_no_warning SinkPlain.bs mkReqSinkPlain

    # The split cone's pinout:
    find_regexp mkReqSinkSplit.v {input  \[31 : 0\] put_1_addr;}
    find_regexp mkReqSinkSplit.v {input  \[63 : 0\] put_1_dat;}

    # Desired: ONE pinout per interface type, program-wide -- the identical
    # module body must produce the same (split) pinout here too.  FAILS
    # today: mkReqSinkPlain.v has a single unsplit port put_1[95:0].
    find_regexp mkReqSinkPlain.v {put_1_addr}

    # One program instantiates both.  bsc's own (typed) connections stay
    # self-consistent, which is exactly what hides the divergence (asserted
    # PASS below); desired is that NO unsplit connection of a Req port
    # exists anywhere in the program.  FAILS today: sysBothSinks.v
    # instantiates mkReqSinkPlain with an unsplit .put_1(...) connection.
    compile_verilog_pass_no_warning BothSinks.bs sysBothSinks
    find_regexp sysBothSinks.v {\.put_1_addr\(}
    find_regexp_fail sysBothSinks.v {\.put_1\(}
}
